{
  "response": "# Accessibility Design Review: ProductNavigation\n\n**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\n**Overall Assessment**:  \nThe component commits a fundamental architectural anti-pattern: using the WAI-ARIA Tabs pattern (`role=\"tablist\"`, `role=\"tab\"`, `role=\"tabpanel\"`) and roving tabindex keyboard navigation for page-level routing (`router.push`). While visually styled as tabs, these controls are functionally navigation links to distinct URLs. Implementing them as ARIA tabs with immediate arrow-key route transitions breaks core browser navigation paradigms (Cmd/Ctrl-click, bookmarks, history stack), misinforms assistive technology users about page context, and introduces potential keyboard unreachability when routes don't match.\n\n---\n\n### Pre-commitment Predictions\n* **Prediction 1**: Route-changing controls implemented as `<button role=\"tab\">` will break native link affordances (open in new tab, context menu, URL preview). *(Confirmed)*\n* **Prediction 2**: Arrow keys will trigger automatic page/route transitions instead of moving focus, cluttering browser history. *(Confirmed)*\n* **Prediction 3**: Roving tabindex will break or trap focus if the route does not match any predefined tab path. *(Confirmed)*\n* **Prediction 4**: `aria-selected` will be used instead of `aria-current=\"page\"`, misrepresenting navigation as in-page state. *(Confirmed)*\n* **Prediction 5**: `role=\"tabpanel\"` will create an unnecessary redundant tab stop around page content. *(Confirmed)*\n\n---\n\n### Critical Findings (Blocks Access)\n\n#### 1. Incomplete Route Matching Renders Navigation Completely Unreachable via Keyboard\n* **Location**: `ProductNavigation.jsx:12, 50`\n* **Element / Pattern**: `const activeIndex = sections.findIndex(s => pathname.startsWith(s.path));` and `tabIndex={index === activeIndex ? 0 : -1}`\n* **Impacted User Group**: Keyboard-only users, switch device users, screen reader users navigating via Tab.\n* **WCAG / APG Citation**: **WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A)**; **WCAG 2.4.3 Focus Order (Level A)**.\n* **Confidence**: HIGH\n* **Why This Matters**: If a user lands on the base route `/product`, an untracked child route (e.g., `/product/checkout`), or any URL with query parameters/canonical variation where `pathname.startsWith(s.path)` returns false for all entries, `activeIndex` evaluates to `-1`. Under the roving tabindex pattern implemented here, **every single tab receives `tabIndex={-1}`**. No element in the tablist has `tabIndex=\"0\"`. Consequently, keyboard users tabbing into the page will completely bypass the product navigation without any way to enter or focus the controls.\n* **Fix**: Replace the entire roving tabindex tablist pattern with a standard semantic `<nav>` list containing native `<a>` (or Next.js `<Link>`) elements, where all links remain naturally focusable via Tab (`tabIndex={0}`).\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: a11y-tab-unreachable-negative-index\nfingerprint: c4f891a27e3d1b09\nsource: ProductNavigation.jsx:12-50\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard, WCAG 2.4.3 Focus Order\nsection_508_fpc_context: WCAG 2.0 2.1.1 (Level A)\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: keyboard-only=HIGH, screen-reader=HIGH\nevidence: ProductNavigation.jsx:12 `const activeIndex = sections.findIndex(...)`; Line 50 `tabIndex={index === activeIndex ? 0 : -1}`. When pathname === '/product', activeIndex === -1, causing all 4 tabs to render with tabIndex=\"-1\".\nreproduction_steps: 1. Navigate to `/product`. 2. Press Tab repeatedly. 3. Observe that focus jumps past all tabs directly to the tabpanel.\nexpected_behavior: Navigation items must always be reachable via the standard keyboard sequential navigation order.\nactual_behavior: The entire navigation bar is skipped by keyboard focus because all items have tabIndex=\"-1\".\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (Significantly Degrades Experience)\n\n#### 1. Arrow Key Handler Triggers Unexpected Route Changes and Pollutes History\n* **Location**: `ProductNavigation.jsx:14-38` (`handleKeyDown`)\n* **Element / Pattern**: `ArrowRight`, `ArrowLeft`, `Home`, `End` handlers invoking `router.push()`\n* **Impacted User Group**: Keyboard-only users, screen reader users, motor-impaired users.\n* **WCAG / APG Citation**: **WCAG 3.2.1 On Focus (Level A)**; **WCAG 3.2.2 On Input (Level A)**; **WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG) - Tabs vs. Navigation**.\n* **Confidence**: HIGH\n* **Why This Matters**: Arrow keys in a tablist are specified to move *focus* (or switch local in-page views). Here, simply tapping `ArrowRight` executes `router.push()`, initiating a full client-side route change and pushing a new entry to the browser's session history for every arrow press. If a user presses `ArrowRight` three times to get to \"Support\", three distinct history entries are committed. Pressing the browser's Back button does not return them to where they came from; it walks back through each intermediate section. Furthermore, triggering async page loads on basic directional navigation causes disorienting re-renders and potential focus displacement mid-keystroke.\n* **Fix**: Remove arrow-key route triggering. Use standard sequential `Tab` navigation across links. If arrow navigation is strictly required within a toolbar/menubar, it must move focus *only*\u2014never execute navigation actions without explicit activation via `Enter`.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: a11y-tab-auto-navigation-on-arrow\nfingerprint: d7e21a998b3c4f21\nsource: ProductNavigation.jsx:14-38\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 3.2.1 On Focus, WCAG 3.2.2 On Input\nsection_508_fpc_context: WCAG 2.0 3.2.1 (Level A)\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: keyboard-only=HIGH, cognitive=HIGH, screen-reader=MEDIUM\nevidence: ProductNavigation.jsx:18, 23, 27, 31: `router.push(...)` called directly on ArrowRight/ArrowLeft/Home/End events.\nreproduction_steps: 1. Focus first tab. 2. Press ArrowRight three times. 3. Check browser history stack and observe 3 route pushes.\nexpected_behavior: Directional keys in composite widgets should either move focus or cycle local views, never trigger page navigations automatically.\nactual_behavior: Directional keystrokes commit route transitions and history mutations.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n#### 2. False ARIA Roles Violate First Rule of ARIA and Destroy Native Link Functionality\n* **Location**: `ProductNavigation.jsx:42-56`\n* **Element / Pattern**: `<div role=\"tablist\">` and `<button role=\"tab\" onClick={() => router.push(section.path)}>`\n* **Impacted User Group**: Screen reader users, keyboard users, all browser users.\n* **WCAG / APG Citation**: **WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A)**; **WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships (Level A)**; **First Rule of ARIA**.\n* **Confidence**: HIGH\n* **Why This Matters**: The First Rule of ARIA states: *\"If you can use a native HTML element or attribute with the semantics and behavior you require already built in, instead of re-purposing an element and adding an ARIA role, state or property to make it accessible, then do so.\"* These controls navigate to separate URLs\u2014they are links, not buttons. Using `<button role=\"tab\">` with `router.push()` strips:\n  - Middle-click / Cmd-click / Ctrl-click to open in a new tab\n  - Right-click context menu options (\"Open link in new window\", \"Copy link address\")\n  - Browser status-bar URL preview on hover/focus\n  - Search engine crawler discoverability\n  - Screen reader announcements of target type (users are told \"tab\" instead of \"link\", expecting in-page panel swaps).\n* **Fix**: Demolish the `role=\"tablist\"` / `role=\"tab\"` markup. Replace with `<nav aria-label=\"Product\">` containing an unordered list `<ul>` of Next.js `<Link>` elements.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: a11y-tab-fake-buttons-for-links\nfingerprint: 8e55bc104a7719d3\nsource: ProductNavigation.jsx:42-56\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value, WAI-ARIA First Rule\nsection_508_fpc_context: WCAG 2.0 4.1.2 (Level A)\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: screen-reader=HIGH, keyboard-only=HIGH, low-vision=LOW\nevidence: ProductNavigation.jsx:44-55: `<button role=\"tab\" onClick={() => router.push(section.path)}>` used to navigate between distinct page URLs.\nreproduction_steps: 1. Right-click any tab or middle-click. 2. Observe lack of \"Open Link in New Tab\" and lack of status bar URL preview.\nexpected_behavior: Page-level destinations must be native HTML anchor links (`<a>` or `<Link>`).\nactual_behavior: Interactive controls are buttons with custom JavaScript routing masquerading as tabs.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n#### 3. Misleading State Communication: `aria-selected` Instead of `aria-current=\"page\"`\n* **Location**: `ProductNavigation.jsx:48`\n* **Element / Pattern**: `aria-selected={index === activeIndex}`\n* **Impacted User Group**: Screen reader users.\n* **WCAG / APG Citation**: **WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A)**; **WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships (Level A)**.\n* **Confidence**: HIGH\n* **Why This Matters**: `aria-selected` communicates selection state within a single-page composite widget (e.g., a tab in a tabbed panel, a cell in a grid). For page-level navigation, assistive technology relies on `aria-current=\"page\"`. When a VoiceOver or NVDA user hears *\"Overview, selected, tab, 1 of 4\"*, they reasonably assume that activating or navigating to another tab will swap content locally in the DOM below without losing their current page landmark structure. Announcing `aria-selected` when a full route transition occurs creates cognitive disorientation.\n* **Fix**: Use `aria-current={index === activeIndex ? \"page\" : undefined}` on native `<a>` / `<Link>` elements.\n\n#### 4. Redundant and Confusing Tab Stop on `role=\"tabpanel\"`\n* **Location**: `ProductNavigation.jsx:60-67`\n* **Element / Pattern**: `<div role=\"tabpanel\" tabIndex={0} ...>`\n* **Impacted User Group**: Keyboard-only users.\n* **WCAG / APG Citation**: **WCAG 2.4.3 Focus Order (Level A)**.\n* **Confidence**: HIGH\n* **Why This Matters**: In a true in-page tab widget, `tabIndex={0}` on `role=\"tabpanel\"` is recommended when the panel contains non-interactive reading content, providing a landing spot for keyboard users. However, here the tabpanel wraps the entire page route content (`product-content`). Forcing keyboard users to land on an arbitrary non-interactive outer container div on every tab pass adds an unnecessary, confusing focus stop before they can reach headings, interactive elements, or main content landmarks.\n* **Fix**: Remove `role=\"tabpanel\"`, `aria-labelledby`, and `tabIndex={0}` entirely. Wrap page content in a semantic `<main id=\"main-content\">` landmark.\n\n---\n\n### Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|:---|:---:|:---|\n| **Screen reader** | **HIGH** | Fabricated ARIA tab roles mask real page navigation; missing `aria-current=\"page\"`; missing `<nav>` landmark. |\n| **Keyboard-only** | **HIGH** | Arrow keys trigger route navigation and history pollution; roving tabindex drops all tabs when path doesn't match (`tabIndex={-1}`). |\n| **Cognitive** | **MEDIUM** | History stack bloat from arrow keys breaks standard Back button mental model; unpredictable route changes. |\n| **Low vision** | **LOW** | Outline indicator exists (3.87:1 against background), but -2px offset sits flush inside active border. |\n| **Vestibular & motion** | **LOW** | No animations or auto-playing motion present in navigation bar. |\n| **Auditory access** | **LOW** | No audio or video content present in component. |\n| **Environmental contrast**| **MEDIUM** | Inactive tab text `#6b7280` on `#f9fafb` produces ~4.6:1 contrast (barely passes 4.5:1 AA threshold). |\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings (Friction but Workaround Exists)\n\n1. **Inactive Tab Contrast is Borderline (4.6:1)**\n   * **Location**: CSS `.product-nav-tab` (`color: #6b7280` on `background: #f9fafb`)\n   * **WCAG Citation**: **WCAG 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum) (Level AA)**\n   * **Issue**: The contrast ratio is approximately 4.6:1. While technically meeting the 4.5:1 threshold, any screen glare or low-quality monitor can degrade readability for low vision users.\n   * **Fix**: Darken inactive text to `#4b5563` (producing 7.3:1 contrast against `#f9fafb`).\n\n2. **Inset Focus Outline Clashes with Active Border**\n   * **Location**: CSS `.product-nav-tab:focus` (`outline-offset: -2px`)\n   * **WCAG Citation**: **WCAG 2.4.11 Focus Appearance (Level AAA / 2.4.7 AA)**\n   * **Issue**: An offset of `-2px` draws the blue outline (`#3b82f6`) directly inside the active bottom border (`#1d4ed8`), reducing focus visibility against the active indicator.\n   * **Fix**: Change `outline-offset` to `2px` so the outline sits cleanly outside the button bounds.\n\n---\n\n### Enhancements (Best Practice Not Met)\n\n1. **Missing `<nav>` Landmark Region**\n   * Wrap the navigation bar in `<nav aria-label=\"Product sections\">`. Landmarks enable screen reader users to jump directly to primary sections via landmark shortcut keys (e.g., `D` in JAWS/NVDA).\n2. **Missing Skip-to-Content Compatibility**\n   * Ensure the destination pages contain a `<main id=\"main-content\">` and that the application header includes a skip link targeting it.\n\n---\n\n### What's Missing (Gaps & Unhandled Edge Cases)\n\n1. **Missing Native Link Capabilities**: Inability to open tabs in new windows/tabs, copy link targets, or bookmark specific tabs via browser context menus.\n2. **Missing SPA Route Focus Management**: When `router.push()` triggers a Next.js route change, focus remains stranded on the unmounted/re-rendered button instead of shifting focus to the page's top-level `<h1>` or `<main>` landmark.\n3. **Missing Graceful Fallback**: If JavaScript fails to load or execute, the entire navigation fails because `<button onClick>` cannot navigate without client-side JS. Native `<Link href>` / `<a href>` works without client-side JS.\n\n---\n\n### Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n* **Screen Reader User**: Hears \"tablist, 4 tabs\". When selecting a tab, hears \"tab selected\", but the entire page URL changes and new page content mounts. Because there is no `<nav>` landmark, navigating by landmarks misses this block entirely.\n* **Keyboard-Only User**: Attempts to explore the tabs using `ArrowRight` (as taught by WAI-ARIA tab specs) and inadvertently triggers 3 client-side page loads and pushes 3 new entries to browser history. If they hit the page on `/product`, they cannot focus the tabs at all because all tabs have `tabIndex=\"-1\"`.\n* **Low Vision User**: Inactive tab text has low visual hierarchy against the light-gray container. The focus indicator with `-2px` offset overlaps the bottom border indicator.\n* **Cognitive Accessibility**: Pressing Back in the browser does not take the user back to the previous website or search results; instead, it replays every arrow-key press the user made while browsing the tabs.\n\n---\n\n### Verdict Justification\n\n**Why REJECT**:  \nThe designer fell into the classic **\"Visual Design \u2260 Semantic Interaction\"** trap. \n\nThe WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices Guide explicitly states:\n> *\"A tab list is for switching between views within the same page without navigating away or reloading. If activating an element navigates to a new URL, it is a **link**, not a tab.\"*\n\nBy forcing the WAI-ARIA Tabs pattern onto what is fundamentally a **site navigation bar**:\n1. It breaks native web link functionality (opening in new tabs, sharing links, crawler indexing).\n2. It breaks browser history and navigation expectations via automatic arrow-key routing.\n3. It creates critical keyboard failure states (`tabIndex={-1}` across all tabs on path mismatch).\n\nVisual styling (a horizontal bar with an active underline indicator) should be achieved using **CSS on semantic HTML links**, not by misapplying ARIA widgets.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Replacement Code\n\n```jsx\nimport React from 'react';\nimport Link from 'next/link';\nimport { usePathname } from 'next/navigation';\n\nconst sections = [\n  { id: 'overview', label: 'Overview', path: '/product/overview' },\n  { id: 'specs', label: 'Specifications', path: '/product/specs' },\n  { id: 'reviews', label: 'Reviews', path: '/product/reviews' },\n  { id: 'support', label: 'Support', path: '/product/support' },\n];\n\nexport const ProductNavigation = () => {\n  const pathname = usePathname();\n\n  return (\n    <nav className=\"product-nav-container\" aria-label=\"Product sections\">\n      <ul className=\"product-nav-list\">\n        {sections.map((section) => {\n          const isCurrent = pathname.startsWith(section.path);\n          return (\n            <li key={section.id} className=\"product-nav-item\">\n              <Link\n                href={section.path}\n                className={`product-nav-link ${isCurrent ? 'active' : ''}`}\n                aria-current={isCurrent ? 'page' : undefined}\n              >\n                {section.label}\n              </Link>\n            </li>\n          );\n        })}\n      </ul>\n    </nav>\n  );\n};\n\nexport default ProductNavigation;\n```\n\n#### Accompanying Clean CSS\n\n```css\n.product-nav-container {\n  max-width: 960px;\n  margin: 0 auto;\n}\n\n.product-nav-list {\n  display: flex;\n  list-style: none;\n  margin: 0;\n  padding: 0 16px;\n  border-bottom: 2px solid #e5e7eb;\n  background: #f9fafb;\n}\n\n.product-nav-item {\n  margin: 0;\n}\n\n.product-nav-link {\n  display: inline-block;\n  padding: 14px 24px;\n  text-decoration: none;\n  color: #4b5563; /* Improved from #6b7280 for 7.3:1 contrast */\n  font-size: 15px;\n  font-weight: 500;\n  border-bottom: 3px solid transparent;\n  margin-bottom: -2px;\n  white-space: nowrap;\n}\n\n.product-nav-link:hover {\n  color: #1f2937;\n  background: #f3f4f6;\n}\n\n.product-nav-link.active {\n  color: #1d4ed8;\n  border-bottom-color: #1d4ed8;\n  font-weight: 600;\n}\n\n.product-nav-link:focus-visible {\n  outline: 2px solid #2563eb;\n  outline-offset: 2px; /* Sits cleanly outside without colliding with border */\n  border-radius: 4px;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Open Questions (Unscored)\n* Is there a requirement for client-side focus shifting on route navigation (e.g., focusing a target heading `<h1>` in `/product/specs` once mounted)? If so, a route change focus manager should be introduced at the template/layout level.\n",
